<p>Some would call the United States of America the mightiest empire the world has ever known. Yet over the past half-century or so, it has shown a strange inability to build domestic infrastructure, at least at the efficiency or on the scale seen elsewhere in the world. (This will not come as news to American enthusiasts of high-speed rail.) Here on Open Culture, we’ve previously featured history <a href="https://www.openculture.com/2024/04/is-america-declining-like-ancient-rome.html">YouTuber Garrett Ryan’s take</a> on the question of whether the U.S. is now declining like the ancient Roman Empire. On <a href="https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=qJIDOq0ezTs">a more recent video</a> for his channel <em>Told in Stone,</em> he’s highlighted the megaprojects that even the Romans — who managed the Pantheon, the Colosseum, and the <a href="https://www.openculture.com/?p=1125042">Pont du Gard</a>, to say nothing of their <a href="https://www.openculture.com/2023/01/the-mystery-finally-solved-why-has-roman-concrete-been-so-durable.html">uniquely long-lasting concrete</a> itself — couldn’t pull off.</p>
<p>The Tiber may seem like a tame river today, but it wasn’t always so placid. In antiquity, rains caused it to flood every year. Senator-historian <a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Cassius_Dio">Cassius Dio</a> recorded an incident in 54 BC when the suddenly rising waters caused not just houses but whole neighborhoods to collapse. The Tiber’s flooding worsened over time, inspiring Julius Caesar to propose re-routing it either around Rome or into a tunnel running beneath the city. Unfortunately, the Ides of March came before work could get underway. Augustus opted to dredge the river channel, and Tiberius championed diverting its tributaries. Only the construction of modern embankments in the nineteenth century brought the regular fluvial disasters under control, ensuring peace for strollers in search of their own <em>dolce vita</em>.</p>
